      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-79606",
        "knowledge_point_id": "68148c93-9139-5deb-8a53-a05d63ec5930",
        "knowledge_point": "Using Linearity Properties of Linear Differential Operators",
        "problem": "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})={x}^{2}+3x+5$ and $L({y}_{2})=x+2$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L({y}_{1}-3{y}_{2})?$",
        "worked_solution": "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L({y}_{1}-3{y}_{2}) & =L({y}_{1})-3L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+3x+5-3(x+2) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+3x+5-3x-6 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
